INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "European Parliament/SAFE policy briefing and Wirecard AG KPMG announcement", "source_url": "SRC-002; SRC-001", "text": "SRC-002 (European Parliament ECON-commission study, November 2020; manuscript completed May 2020) states: Wirecard founded 1999, listed 2005, moved to DAX in 2018. Allegations of accounting inconsistencies began in 2015, including Financial Times reporting based partly on Singapore whistleblower information. The 2016 Zatarra Report accused Wirecard of money laundering and fraud. After a January 2019 FT report, BaFin requested a FREP investigation; BaFin prohibited increases in net short positions for two months and filed criminal complaints against short sellers and the FT journalist. FT continued reporting allegedly inflated sales and profit numbers backed by internal documents. Wirecard hired KPMG in October 2019 for a forensic investigation; the report was delayed until April 2020 and stated KPMG could not verify existence of €1 billion in foreign bank accounts. BaFin filed a criminal complaint against Wirecard in June 2020; days later Wirecard filed for bankruptcy acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. The authors assess that all five lines of defence—internal controls, supervisory board, external audit, reporting/audit oversight bodies, and BaFin—failed in one way or another, while noting hindsight-bias concerns and framing this as an assessment, not a court finding. SRC-001 is Wirecard's 28 April 2020 announcement: it said KPMG found no incriminating evidence of balance-sheet forgery and no significant findings requiring adjustment of 2016–18 accounts; it also said not all data needed to prove TPA revenues could be obtained because data were controlled by third parties, documented organizational weaknesses existed, and E&Y audit timing remained unresolved."}
